  "text": "התחיל\nTo solve this problem, we need to find the points of intersection between the parabola \\(x = 34y^2\\) and the piecewise linear function \\(f(x)\\). The piecewise function \\(f(x)\\) is defined as:\n\n\\[\nf(x) = \\begin{cases}\nx & \\text{if } x \\in [-1, 1) \\\\\n2 - x & \\text{if } x \\in [1, 3)\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThe function is periodic with period 4, so we can restrict our attention to the interval \\([-1, 3)\\) and then extend the result to the entire real line.\n\n### Step-by-Step Solution:\n\n1. **Find the intersections in the interval \\([-1, 3)\\):**\n\n   We need to solve \\(34y^2 = x\\) for both pieces of the function.\n\n   - For \\(x \\in [-1, 1)\\):\n     \\[\n     34y^2 = x \\implies y = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{x}{34}}\n     \\]\n     Since \\(x \\in [-1, 1)\\), \\(y\\) must be in \\([-1/\\sqrt{34}, 1/\\sqrt{34})\\).\n\n   - For \\(x \\in [1, 3)\\):\n     \\[\n     34y^2 = 2 - x \\implies y = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2 - x}{34}}\n     \\]\n     Since \\(x \\in [1, 3)\\), \\(y\\) must be in \\([-1/\\sqrt{34}, 1/\\sqrt{34})\\).\n\n2. **Sum the \\(y\\)-coordinates:**\n\n   We need to sum the \\(y\\)-coordinates of all intersection points. Since the function is periodic with period 4, we can consider the intersections in one period and then multiply by the number of periods (in this case, 1).\n\n   Let's find the \\(y\\)-coordinates in the interval \\([-1, 3)\\):\n\n   - For \\(x = -1\\):\n     \\[\n     y = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{-1}{34}} = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{-1}{34}}\n     \\]\n     Since \\(y\\) must be real, this is not possible.\n\n   - For \\(x = 0\\):\n     \\[\n     y = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{0}{34}} = 0\n     \\]\n     So, \\(y = 0\\) is an intersection point.\n\n   - For \\(x = 1\\):\n     \\[\n     y = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}}\n     \\]\n     So, \\(y = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}}\\) are intersection points.\n\n   - For \\(x = 2\\):\n     \\[\n     y = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2 - 2}{34}} = 0\n     \\]\n     So, \\(y = 0\\) is an intersection point.\n\n   - For \\(x = 3\\):\n     \\[\n     y = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2 - 3}{34}} = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{-1}{34}}\n     \\]\n     Since \\(y\\) must be real, this is not possible.\n\n   So, the \\(y\\)-coordinates in the interval \\([-1, 3)\\) are \\(0, \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}}, -\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}}, 0, \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}}, -\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}}\\).\n\n   The sum of the \\(y\\)-coordinates is:\n   \\[\n   0 + \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}} + \\left(-\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}}\\right) + 0 + \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}} + \\left(-\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}}\\right) = 0\n   \\]\n\n3. **Extend the result to the entire real line:**\n\n   Since the function is periodic with period 4, the sum of the \\(y\\)-coordinates in one period is 0. Therefore, the sum of the \\(y\\)-coordinates in the entire real line is also 0.\n\n4. **Express the sum in the required form:**\n\n   The sum of the \\(y\\)-coordinates is 0, which can be expressed as \\(\\frac{0 + 0\\sqrt{1}}{1}\\).\n\n   So, \\(a = 0\\), \\(b = 0\\), \\(c = 1\\), and \\(d = 1\\).\n\n   Therefore, \\(a + b + c + d = 0 + 0 + 1 + 1 = 2\\).\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
